## corpus/faults/assert_msg_effect_fails.lu
//! check: run(exit=trap(assert), stdout="effect\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: conf.trap.assert
//!
//! s202 (wolf-lang#398), the other direction: an interpolation whose
//! hole CALLS was accepted by native and release and dropped
//! unevaluated, so on the failing path `effect` never printed, while
//! checked and lupin print it. The message is evaluated on the
//! failing path, before the trap, on every lane.
## corpus/faults/assert_msg_name_fails.lu
//! check: run(exit=trap(assert), stdout="before\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: conf.trap.assert
//!
//! s202 (wolf-lang#398): the failing twin of `assert_msg_name.lu`.
//! The message is a bare parameter and the condition is the constant
//! `false`, so the trap is unconditional and the message is read on
//! the way to it. Native and release refused the program at trunk
//! 12a56b22. lupin renders the message as a line before the trap,
//! which `[conf.trap.assert]` allows and does not require; the
//! compiler drops the rendering, never the evaluation.
## corpus/faults/assert_msg_name.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="ok\nafter\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: conf.trap.assert
//!
//! s202 (wolf-lang#398): any `str` is an assert message
//! (`[conf.trap.assert]`), and it is evaluated ONLY on the failing
//! path. The issue's helper passes its message as a bare parameter;
//! a field read is the same kind of message. Native and release
//! refused both as "assert messages with effects" because the check
//! was by syntax kind (a name is not a literal), while checked and
//! lupin ran them. The holding asserts below run nothing: `noisy()`
//! never prints, whether the message is the call itself or an
//! interpolation of it.
## corpus/faults/assert_msg_try.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="7\n-1\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: conf.trap.assert, err.propagate
//!
//! s202 (wolf-lang#398): a message that can leave the function. On the
//! failing path `why(n)?` propagates `nope` before any trap, so
//! `check(1)` returns the row and the caller's `else` answers `-1`; on
//! the holding path the message is never evaluated. Native and
//! release refused this at trunk 12a56b22; checked and lupin ran it.
## corpus/memory/ctl_index_else_once.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="value\nidx\n20\nidx\n10\nreturn\nidx\n20\nidx\n9\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: mem.model.order, mem.model.place
//!
//! s189 (wolf-lang#481) — `else` in an index operand. `xs[idx() else 0]`
//! handles the row inside the operand, so the operand yields a value
//! and runs once on every machine (a control). `xs[idx() else { return
//! 9 }]` leaves the function from inside the operand: it runs once and
//! the `return` wins. The checked machine ran it twice.
## corpus/memory/ctl_index_return_break_once.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="return\nidx\n20\nidx\n9\nbreak\nidx\n10\nidx\ndone\ncontinue\nidx\n10\nidx\nidx\n30\ndone\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: mem.model.order, mem.model.place
//!
//! s189 (wolf-lang#481) — `return`, `break` and `continue` inside an
//! index operand: the operand runs once and the flow leaves as it would
//! from any other operand. The checked machine dropped the flow as "not
//! a place" and ran the operand again.
## corpus/memory/ctl_index_try_once.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="let\nidx\n20\nidx\n9\nhole\nidx\n20\n0\nidx\n9\narg\nidx\n20\n0\nidx\n9\nmember\nidx\n2\nidx\n9\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: mem.model.order, mem.model.place
//!
//! s189 (wolf-lang#481) — an index operand whose `?` propagates runs
//! once, wherever the indexed place stands: bound by `let`, inside an
//! interpolation hole, as a `read` argument, and as a member base. The
//! checked machine evaluated the operand, dropped its flow as "not a
//! place", and evaluated the whole expression again: `idx` ran two or
//! three times. Native, release and lupin run it once.
## corpus/memory/ctl_index_try_once_receivers.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="copy\nidx\n9\ncount\nidx\n9\nfor\nidx\n9\nelemcopy\nidx\n5\nidx\n9\npush\nidx\n3\nidx\n9\nbytes\nidx\n9\nbytesfor\nidx\n9\nstrlen\nidx\n9\nprint\nidx\ncde\n0\nidx\n9\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: mem.model.order, mem.model.place
//!
//! s189 (wolf-lang#481) — an index operand whose `?` propagates, under
//! the other readers of a place: `copy`, a method receiver (`count`,
//! `(mut …).push`), a `for` iterable, the right-hand side of an element
//! store, a `str` element's byte view (`.bytes().len`, `for b in
//! ….bytes()`), a `str` element's `.len`, and a `print` argument. The
//! operand runs once in each. The checked machine ran it three times,
//! six under a byte view. lupin 0.1.42 runs it twice as a method
//! receiver, under a byte view and under a `str` member (the driver
//! gate pins that answer by version).
